.TH FEGETEXCEPTFLAG 3 "2026-05-03" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
fegetexceptflag, fesetexceptflag \- get and set floating-point exception flags
.SH LIBRARY
Math Library (libm, \-lm)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <fenv.h>
.PP
.BI "int fegetexceptflag(fexcept_t *" flagp ", int " excepts );
.BI "int fesetexceptflag(const fexcept_t *" flagp ", int " excepts );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR fegetexceptflag ()
and
.BR fesetexceptflag ()
functions form a paired save/restore API for floating-point exception sticky
flags.
They allow a program to snapshot a subset of the exception flags and restore
them later without affecting the rounding mode or exception masks.
.SS fegetexceptflag
.BR fegetexceptflag ()
reads the current floating-point exception flags indicated by
.I excepts
and stores them in the object pointed to by
.IR flagp .
The stored value is an implementation-defined representation of those flags;
it must be treated as opaque and may only be passed to
.BR fesetexceptflag ().
.SS fesetexceptflag
.BR fesetexceptflag ()
restores the exception flags indicated by
.I excepts
from the object pointed to by
.IR flagp ,
which must have been previously set by a call to
.BR fegetexceptflag ()
with the same or a superset of
.IR excepts .
Only the bits named in
.I excepts
are modified; flags not included in
.I excepts
are left unchanged.
.PP
.BR fesetexceptflag ()
sets the sticky bits directly in the FPU status word or MXCSR.
It does
.I not
raise floating-point traps, even if the corresponding exception is unmasked.
This distinguishes it from
.BR feraiseexcept (3),
which does trigger traps.
.PP
The
.I excepts
argument is a bitwise OR of zero or more of the following macros:
.BR FE_INVALID ,
.BR FE_DIVBYZERO ,
.BR FE_OVERFLOW ,
.BR FE_UNDERFLOW ,
.BR FE_INEXACT ,
or
.BR FE_ALL_EXCEPT .
.SH RETURN VALUE
Both functions return zero on success.
A non-zero return value indicates failure (for example, an unsupported flag
bit was specified in
.IR excepts ).
.SH ERRORS
No errors are defined.
Neither function sets
.IR errno .
.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates saving the exception flags before a
computation, clearing them, performing the computation, testing for new
exceptions, and then restoring the original flags:
.PP
.nf
#pragma STDC FENV_ACCESS ON
#include <fenv.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>

int main(void)
{
    fexcept_t saved;

    /* Snapshot the current flags for FE_INVALID and FE_DIVBYZERO. */
    fegetexceptflag(&saved, FE_INVALID | FE_DIVBYZERO);

    /* Clear those flags before the computation. */
    feclearexcept(FE_INVALID | FE_DIVBYZERO);

    /* Computation that might raise FE_INVALID (sqrt of negative). */
    volatile double v = -1.0;
    volatile double r = sqrt(v);
    (void)r;

    if (fetestexcept(FE_INVALID))
        printf("FE_INVALID raised during computation.\\n");

    /* Restore original flags (does NOT trigger traps). */
    fesetexceptflag(&saved, FE_INVALID | FE_DIVBYZERO);

    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fenv (7),
.BR feclearexcept (3),
.BR feraiseexcept (3),
.BR fetestexcept (3)
